hypotheses.statement:
The map $\mathfrak t_q(k)\longrightarrow\mathfrak t_{q-1}(k)$ which forgets the last strand is split, and its kernel is the free ordinary Lie algebra on $s_i=t_{iq}$, $1\leq i<q$. Explicitly, $$\label{eq:free-fiber}
 \mathfrak t_q(k)
  =\operatorname{Lie}_k\langle s_1,\ldots,s_{q-1}\rangle
        \rtimes\mathfrak t_{q-1}(k),$$ where a base generator $t_{ij}$ acts by the derivation $$\label{eq:free-fiber-action}
 D_{ij}(s_i)=[s_i,s_j],\qquad
 D_{ij}(s_j)=[s_j,s_i],\qquad
 D_{ij}(s_h)=0\quad(h\notin\{i,j\}).$$ Multiplication gives an isomorphism of $k$-modules $$\label{eq:enveloping-fiber}
 k\langle s_1,\ldots,s_{q-1}\rangle
       \otimes_k U(\mathfrak t_{q-1}(k))
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }U(\mathfrak t_q(k)).$$ Thus recursive products of free associative fiber words are a basis. Over $R$, the Lie algebra and its enveloping algebra are torsion free, and this normal form commutes with rationalization and reduction.

Let $\psi\in W_n$, where $n>2$. In the free Lie algebra on $a,b$, with $c=-a-b$, one has $$\label{eq:special}
 [a,\psi(b,a)]+[c,\psi(b,c)]=0.$$ In addition, for an extra free letter $T$, $$\label{eq:special-linearization}
 \partial_2\psi(a,-a)T=0,$$ where $\partial_2$ means the coefficient of a central parameter $t$ in $\psi(a,-a+tT)$.

In the integral generator basis $a_i=t_{z_i=0}$, $u_{ij}=t_{z_i-z_j=0}$, $v_{ij}=t_{z_i-z_j=0}+t_{z_i+z_j=0}$, the complete nonzero edge weights are the following linear functionals: $$\label{eq:deletion-edge-table}
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{edge}&\text{$a,v$ part}&\text{$u$ part}\\ \hline
0p&0&u_{23}^{*}\\
0w&0&u_{12}^{*}\\
0e&0&0\\
ep&a_3^{*}-v_{23}^{*}&0\\
ew&a_1^{*}-v_{12}^{*}&0\\
pw&a_2^{*}+v_{13}^{*}-v_{12}^{*}-v_{23}^{*}&u_{13}^{*}
\end{array}$$ Equal-letter edges have weight zero. After reduction modulo two, a word containing $r$ zeros has zero scalar test on every product with fewer than $r$ factors of type $u$. For a product with exactly $r$ such factors, every $u$-factor must delete a zero in any nonzero contribution.

